/* =====================================================================
   DATA — this is the part you own. Edit this file only; index.html
   never needs to change when you add, remove, or fix a resource.

   This map starts scoped to Hernando County, FL, but the schema is built
   to grow — statewide, then national — the same way the cyber resource
   maps did. That's why every row carries `county` and `st` even though
   today they're all "Hernando" / "FL": it's what a future regions.js /
   state drill-down would key off, without ever touching this file's shape.

   How to add a row:
     1. Get lat/lng from a real geocoder (US Census Bureau geocoder —
        geocoding.geo.census.gov — or Nominatim/OpenStreetMap) or by
        right-clicking the spot in Google Maps. NEVER estimate/eyeball a
        coordinate for a resource a homeless or at-risk person might
        actually try to walk or ride a bus to — a wrong pin sends someone
        to the wrong building. Exception: confidential shelter locations
        (domestic-violence shelters) — see Dawn Center below — where the
        pin is deliberately generalized to the city center for safety and
        the notes say so explicitly.
     2. category — one of:
          hub      multi-service anchor (3+ distinct aid types under one
                    roof/org — food + medical + casework, etc.)
          food     food pantries, meal programs, mobile food distribution
          shelter  emergency/transitional shelter, domestic-violence
                    shelter — anywhere with actual overnight beds
          clothing thrift stores / free-clothing & household-goods
                    voucher programs
          medical  free/low-cost clinics
          coalition coordinating/advocacy agencies (CoC lead, task forces)
          transportation bus/paratransit systems
          pet      pet food pantries
          hotline  phone-first intake/crisis lines with no single
                    physical address people visit
          workspace free public workspace — computers, meeting rooms
                    (libraries); also where a real commissary/shared-use
                    kitchen would go if one is ever confirmed inside the
                    county (none found as of this sweep — see README).
                    Dedicated employment/career-services orgs (CareerSource,
                    Vocational Rehab) are `jobs` instead, even though they
                    also offer computers, since job placement is their
                    actual mission
          hygiene  shower access — gyms (by cheapest membership found) and
                    any free public shower option
          camping  legally designated public camping — state forest primitive
                    zones and developed campgrounds. NOT general "public
                    land" — only actual designated, permitted camping
                    areas. WMA land where overnight camping is explicitly
                    NOT permitted (e.g. Chassahowitzka WMA proper) is
                    deliberately excluded, not implied as an option
          jobs     dedicated job/employment centers — CareerSource (the
                    state's official "American Job Center"), Vocational
                    Rehabilitation. Distinct from `workspace`, which is
                    generic free-computer/meeting-room access (libraries);
                    an entry belongs here only if job placement/career
                    services are its actual mission, not just a side
                    effect of having public computers
          benefits government social-welfare/benefits administration —
                    Housing Authority (Section 8), the DCF/ACCESS Florida
                    office (Medicaid, SNAP, TANF applications in person),
                    the school district's federally-mandated homeless
                    student liaison. Distinct from `hotline` — these are
                    physical government offices you can walk into, not
                    phone-first lines with no local address
     3. county / st — "Hernando" / "FL" today; keep populated on every
        row so a future statewide pass can filter/group by it immediately.
     4. url / address / phone / when / org may be "" or omitted if
        unknown — the popup simply omits empty fields. Never guess
        contact details.
     5. services: [...] — OPTIONAL, cross-cutting need tags, independent
        of category (same idea as the cyber map's `topics`). Fixed
        taxonomy: food, shelter, medical, clothing, financial,
        transportation, petfood, snap, veterans, seniors, children,
        casework, legal, dv (domestic violence), housing, energy
        (utility assistance), headstart, mentalhealth, workspace, showers.
        Only tag what's actually confirmed offered — don't guess.
     6. events: [{ title, when, topics:[] }] — OPTIONAL, named programs run
        out of one location on their own schedule (e.g. PHP's Neighborhood
        Blessings vs. its Tuesday medical clinic). Pulled only from the
        org's own published info, never invented.
     7. verified: "YYYY-MM-DD" — OPTIONAL, date this specific row was
        independently confirmed via the org's own site/a fresh search
        (not just carried over from an older compiled list). Rows without
        it should carry a call-ahead caveat in notes instead.
     8. idRequired: plain-language string — REQUIRED on every `shelter`-
        and `food`-category row (optional elsewhere). What someone
        actually needs to show up with, sourced from the org's own site
        where possible. "None published — appears low-barrier" is an
        honest, legitimate value when no requirement could be found;
        never leave it blank and never assume a photo ID is required
        just because that's the norm elsewhere — several of this
        county's shelters explicitly do NOT require one (that's a
        deliberate low-barrier-access
        practice at DV shelters especially, not an oversight).

   Data sweep: August 2026, sixth pass same month — added a `benefits`
   category for government social-welfare administration offices: the
   Hernando County Housing Authority (Section 8 — waitlist reported
   closed at this sweep, opens only periodically, call to check), the
   actual DCF/ACCESS Florida in-person office (distinct from the county
   HHS referral desk — this is where Medicaid/SNAP/TANF applications get
   filed), and the school district's federally-mandated McKinney-Vento
   homeless-student liaison (immediate enrollment, no paperwork required,
   by federal law). Also added Social Security Administration and the
   statewide ACA Marketplace navigator line under `hotline`, since
   neither has a physical Hernando County office.

   Fifth pass same month — added a dedicated
   `jobs` category (CareerSource, reclassified out of `workspace` since
   job placement is its actual mission, plus both of Hernando County's
   Florida Division of Vocational Rehabilitation offices, newly found).
   Goodwill's planned Brooksville job-training center was found but NOT
   added — it doesn't open until late 2027/2028, and this map only lists
   what's actually operating now, same rule applied earlier to EōS
   Fitness's not-yet-open location.

   Fourth pass same month (a full gym sweep —
   every fitness chain/independent gym found in the county, including
   two private country clubs listed for completeness and clearly flagged
   as such — plus a second, deeper church/charity research pass beyond
   the original PHP sheet: 5 new orgs found this pass that PHP's list
   never had — Faith Presbyterian's free showers, M.O.V.E.'s transitional
   housing program, Spring Hill Baptist's clothes closet, St. Frances
   Cabrini's pantry, and VineLife Christian Church's pantry — plus 3
   existing church rows enriched with real addresses/specific ID rules
   found via the churches' own sites rather than the generic county
   pattern). Third pass (same month) added camping and a
   phone-verification confidence pass — no calling capability exists in
   this environment, so "verified" below means cross-checked against an
   independent source, never an actual phone call; two rows carry an
   explicit CONFIDENCE NOTE in their `notes` where only one source could
   be found for a number — Salvation Army's Spring Hill line and Esther's
   House's extension). The county's own
   pantry-sheet source (compiled by People Helping People) is dated
   October 2021 — every church/pantry row sourced ONLY from that sheet
   says so in its notes and has no `verified` date. Call ahead before a
   special trip; volunteer-run pantry schedules drift as churches change
   staff. Anchor orgs and everything added in the second pass (shelters,
   coalitions, workspace, hygiene/gyms, veterans and mental-health
   support) were independently sourced this sweep — via the orgs' own
   sites, the Mid Florida Homeless Coalition, and a congressional-office
   community resource guide (bilirakis.house.gov) — and carry a
   `verified` date. SECOND-PASS CORRECTION: the Salvation Army's Hernando
   County Corps relocated from Brooksville to 342 Beverly Court, Spring
   Hill in July 2025 — the old Cortez Blvd address/hours from the first
   sweep were wrong by the time of this pass; fixed below. Two entries
   (Life Center of Hernando, and the youth crisis shelter run by Youth &
   Family Alternatives) intentionally use a generalized city-center pin
   rather than a residential/shelter street address, same safety pattern
   as the Dawn Center — see their notes.
   ===================================================================== */
